speechtext:
In so far as the Department of Industry, Trade and Commerce is concerned:
1.	"Enterprise 77" is a program which commenced in fiscal year 1977-78.
2.	$1,510,900.
3.	Total cost for 1977-78 $1,441,182 which includes:
(a)	$204,320;
(b)	$401,056;
(c)	$195,448;
(d)	$ 5,195.
4.	The benefits to industry and small business, in particular, accomplished by the Enterprise Canada '77 Project were:
1.	A series of detailed reports on subjects, indicating businessmen's views regarding existing government programs and services useful as the basis of making changes.
2.	Out of the findings, the Department of Industry, Trade and Commerce has:
(a)	developed a business service centre and direct dial system for businessmen to get service regarding any problem they might have with the federal government maze.
(b)	developed a business opportunities sourcing system which, when completed, will be a significant tool for the firm to identify potential sources of supply in Canada, as well as promote their own products to other firms;
(c)	developed the office of the paper burden controller, with the comments of Canadian businessmen as the basis of action;
(d)	provided a useful input for many of the recent policy formulations in support of small/medium business including some tax changes;
(e)	caused a closer review of overlap in federal and provincial programs and services, still ongoing;
(0 affirmed the need to regionalize operations of the department to get closer to its clientele.
3.	Other shorter term benefits to industry and small business, were:
(a)	practical seminars to industry, where requested, on a variety of topics with significant business involvement, including: how to get through U.S. Customs; and select export market seminars;
(b)	a better knowledge of federal and provincial programs and services including promulgation of some 35,000 copies of a book entitled "Canadian Federal Government Services to Business".
Order Paper Questions OMBUDSMAN FOR TRANSPORT CANADA
